We examine a system of $N$ particles, denoted as $x_1^{(N)},\ldots,x_N^{(N)}$, that are governed by the stochastic differential equations:
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{d}x_i^{(N)} = \mathrm{d}B_i^{(N)} + \frac{1}{N}\sum_{j\neq i}^{N}
\frac{1}{|x_i^{(N)}-x_j^{(N)}|^\alpha},
\end{equation*}
where $\alpha\in(0,1)$ and $B_1^{(N)},\ldots,B_N^{(N)}$ are independent Brownian motions.
We demonstrate that the empirical measure of the particles, $\mu^{(N)}$, converges in probability to a solution of the stochastic PDE:
\begin{equation*}
\partial_t u = \Delta u + \mathcal{W}(|\nabla u|^\alpha),
\end{equation*}
where $\mathcal{W}$ represents white noise.
